Fix a nonnegative integer $d$ and real numbers $0\le s<t$. For all sufficiently large $N$, every $U\in\mathcal U_d(N)$ and every pair of counts $M,D$ on $\mathcal H_N$ satisfy $$\label{eq:localization}
 \left\lVert [M\ge N^t]\,U[D=0]U^\dagger[M=0]\right\rVert
 \le \exp(-N^s).$$ The sufficient lower bound on $N$ depends only on $d,s,t$, not on the circuit, the counted subsets, or their reference vectors.

Fix real numbers $0<a<b$ and $u>(a+b)/2$. For every sufficiently large integer $N$, there is a real polynomial $p$ such that $$\label{eq:polynomial-bounds}
  \deg p\le N^u,
  \qquad W_N(p)\le \exp(N^u),
  \qquad p(0)=1,$$ and $$\label{eq:polynomial-decay}
  |p(x)|\le \exp(-N^a)
  \qquad\text{for every integer }1\le x\le \lceil N^b\rceil.$$ The threshold for $N$ depends only on $a,b,u$. No restriction $b\le1$ is imposed.

Fix $d\ge1$ and assume $\mathcal B_{d-1}(s,t)$ for every fixed pair $0\le s<t$. For fixed $\alpha,\sigma\ge0$ and $\beta>\max\{\alpha,\sigma\}$, all sufficiently large $N$ satisfy $$\label{eq:low-count-propagation}
 \left\lVert [M\ge N^\beta]B[M<N^\alpha]\right\rVert
 \le \exp(-N^\sigma)$$ uniformly for every count $M$ and every $B=VP_*V^\dagger$, where $V\in\mathcal U_{d-1}(N)$ and $P_*$ is a product projection.

Fix $d\ge1$ and assume $\mathcal B_{d-1}(s,t)$ for every fixed pair $0\le s<t$. For fixed $u,w\ge0$ and $t>\max\{u,w\}$, all sufficiently large $N$ satisfy $$\label{eq:small-support}
 \left\lVert [M\ge N^t]UAU^\dagger[M=0]\right\rVert
 \le\exp(-N^w)$$ uniformly for every $U\in\mathcal U_d(N)$, every count $M$, and every product projection $A$ supported on at most $N^u$ qubits.

Suppose $\psi_0$ is a normalized product state and its trajectory under $W$ is regular. Let $P_0=|\psi_0\rangle\langle\psi_0|$ be the full product projection and $H_0$ its count. For any product test $B$ at the output and every fixed $0<p<h<1$, $$\label{eq:product-tail}
 \norm{\mathbf 1_{H_0\ge m^h}W^{\dagger}P_BW\psi_0}
 \le \exp(-m^p)
 \quad\text{eventually}.$$

Under the hypotheses of Corollary <a href="#cor:product-tail" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="cor:product-tail">13</a>, write $\psi_t=W\psi_0$. For any product test $B$ at the output and every fixed $0<p<h<1$, $$\label{eq:overlap-tail}
 \norm{P_B\psi_t}^{2}
 \norm{\mathbf 1_{H_B\ge m^h}\psi_t}
 \le \exp(-m^p)
 \quad\text{eventually}.$$ For each fixed $(h,p)$, both this estimate and <a href="#eq:product-tail" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:product-tail">[eq:product-tail]</a> hold eventually uniformly over the output test $B$.
